{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VK9AjCNzEdVtKip286k5aY","type":"tool_result","content":"68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{H\n30\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan20\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. Fluid benchmarking goes further by choosing items adaptively under an item-response model of latent ability \\citep{Hofmann2025Fluid\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the o\n## Repair pass (2026-11-11T13:05Z)\n- Fixed figure_manifest.json to the rich schema shape (both figures; protocol_overview kept as generate-concept-figure with prompt log).\n- Audit fixes: sealed configuration identity qualified (proposed pair OR latent-skill extension, indistinguishable; tree hash unmatched); removed \"frozen\"/\"evaluated once\"; abstract/conclusion refer to the selector-imputer family; SI hyperparameter table labelled as proposed-pair source copy, not verified as sealed tree; H3 reported as not tested by sealed evaluation (0.016 small/unresolved).\n- claims_grounding.json claims 4 and 7 updated; evidence verifier: 0 dangling. Compiled (13 pages, body ends p.9).\n- Citation gaps for benchmark-level comparators and redundancy were not filled: retrieval hit HTTP 429 and no grounded references were retrieved; Related Work already states latent-factor predictors were not run as baselines. No references invented.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fbba1fb2-6f3b-44fa-8173-06023497607c","uuid":"fb4bac94-1121-4bba-9c2b-68dd8de45e2b","timestamp":"2026-11-11T13:05:43.148Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}\n109:\\paragraph{Data.} Sealed evaluation uses three public leaderboards at pinned revisions: MTEB results (297 benchmark columns; 124 history and 37 held-out models), the Open LLM Leaderboard table (six benchmarks, namely IFEval \\citep{Zhou2023IFEval}, BBH \\citep{Suzgun2023BBH}, MATH Level~5 \\citep{H\n30\n40:A full evaluation of a language or embedding model touches dozens to hundreds of benchmarks, each costing compute, time and human effort. Public leaderboards meanwhile accumulate large model$\\times$benchmark score matrices, and benchmark suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench, HELM and MTEB \\citep{Hendrycks2021MMLU,Srivastava2023BIGbench,Liang2023HELM,Muennighoff2023MTEB} are known to overlap in what they measure: agreement analyses across dozens of benchmarks and low-dimensional capability models fitted to public evaluations both point to strongly related scores \\citep{Perlitz2024BenchBench,Ruan20\n68:\\paragraph{Cheaper evaluation within or across benchmarks.} tinyBenchmarks, anchor points and efficient-benchmarking studies shrink the number of items of a single benchmark while preserving its score or ranking \\citep{Polo2024TinyBenchmarks,Vivek2024AnchorPoints,Perlitz2024EfficientBenchmarking}. metabench distills six benchmarks into a sparse one using the responses of thousands of models and checks recovery of the original scores \\citep{Kipnis2024Metabench}. Fluid benchmarking goes further by choosing items adaptively under an item-response model of latent ability \\citep{Hofmann2025Fluid\n72:\\paragraph{Missing data, matrix completion and design.} Gaussian models with missing entries are fitted by EM \\citep{Dempster1977EM} under missingness assumptions discussed by \\citet{Rubin1976Inference}. Low-rank and probabilistic matrix completion \\citep{Candes2009MatrixCompletion,Salakhutdinov2007PMF} are the standard alternatives for filling score matrices. Selecting observations to minimise posterior uncertainty is an experimental-design problem \\citep{Chaloner1995BayesianDesign}; for Gaussian processes, greedy selection of informative variables has near-optimality guarantees when the o\n## Repair pass (2026-11-11T13:05Z)\n- Fixed figure_manifest.json to the rich schema shape (both figures; protocol_overview kept as generate-concept-figure with prompt log).\n- Audit fixes: sealed configuration identity qualified (proposed pair OR latent-skill extension, indistinguishable; tree hash unmatched); removed \"frozen\"/\"evaluated once\"; abstract/conclusion refer to the selector-imputer family; SI hyperparameter table labelled as proposed-pair source copy, not verified as sealed tree; H3 reported as not tested by sealed evaluation (0.016 small/unresolved).\n- claims_grounding.json claims 4 and 7 updated; evidence verifier: 0 dangling. Compiled (13 pages, body ends p.9).\n- Citation gaps for benchmark-level comparators and redundancy were not filled: retrieval hit HTTP 429 and no grounded references were retrieved; Related Work already states latent-factor predictors were not run as baselines. No references invented.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
